from pathlib import Path
cases={
'paykaro':[
('Problem','A first payment should feel like a small win. In this fictional case, UPI activation was stuck at 41%. People reached the bank-linking step and left, often without knowing whether their money or account was safe. Seven separate screens asked them to make decisions before they understood the payoff.\n\nThe brief was not to make onboarding look shorter. It was to help someone complete a first transaction with confidence.'),
('Research','The supplied demo scenario includes 18 interviews with new and recently inactive users. The recurring questions were practical: “Which bank should I choose?”, “Why does this need my phone number?” and “Did that actually work?”\n\nI grouped the findings into three needs: less repetition, a clear reason for each permission, and visible confirmation after every important step. A journey map made the gap between technical success and perceived success explicit.'),
('Ideation','I explored a single long form, a guided conversation and a three-stage flow. The long form reduced taps but increased uncertainty; the guided flow felt reassuring but slow. The final direction grouped tasks around outcomes: identify the account, connect the bank, make the first payment.\n\nSmart bank-detection became a suggestion with an easy override, never an unexplained automatic choice. Error states were sketched alongside the happy path.'),
('Design','The concept uses progressive disclosure and short, specific explanations. Users see their chosen account before committing, can change the bank without restarting, and receive a clear success state after linking.\n\nThe original mockups below illustrate hierarchy and flow rather than a functioning payment app. Components include accessible input states, connection feedback and a compact receipt. No payments or financial data are collected by this portfolio.'),
('Results','In the fictional brief, activation rises from 41% to 79% after six weeks: **38 percentage points**, not a 38% relative increase. The distinction matters when discussing an outcome. Onboarding falls from seven steps to three.\n\nThese numbers are supplied demo metrics, not independently measured results. A real rollout would compare cohorts and watch unsuccessful links, support contacts and first-payment completion alongside activation.'),
('Key learnings','- Removing a screen does not automatically remove doubt.\n- Suggestions work best when people can correct them.\n- Measure confidence as well as completion.\n- Define the metric before celebrating the change.')],
'greencart':[
('Problem','A full basket was not translating into completed orders. The fictional GreenCart team saw shoppers hesitate when delivery timing, fees and substitutions appeared late in checkout. The interface treated a weekly shop like an impulse purchase.\n\nThe goal was to let households make a clear commitment, with the information that mattered visible before the final step.'),
('Research','For this illustrative case study, I mapped the shopping journey from a saved list to a delivered order. The research plan combined checkout observation, support-theme review and short interviews about the weekly routine.\n\nThe working hypotheses were uncertainty about delivery slots, surprise costs and a loss of context when editing a basket. These are design assumptions for the demo, not claims about a real user study.'),
('Ideation','Three directions tested different priorities: a fastest-path checkout, a detailed order summary and a household-first basket. I chose the last approach, keeping delivery details close to the products and making edits reversible.\n\nA quick comparison matrix helped the team separate a simpler appearance from a genuinely simpler task.'),
('Design','The concept makes the selected delivery window and full total visible early. Quantity controls keep their state when an item is updated. Substitution preferences are expressed in plain language, and a review step leaves room to catch mistakes.\n\nThe original desktop and mobile concepts focus on easy scanning, generous targets and clear product information. They are static design illustrations, not a shopping or payment service.'),
('Results','The supplied fictional outcome is **22% less checkout drop-off**. A real assessment would specify the baseline, time period and eligible sessions before attributing the change to design.\n\nThe supporting measurement plan tracks basket edits, slot availability failures and support requests. A completed order is only a win if the customer receives what they expected.'),
('Key learnings','- Make commitments visible before asking for a decision.\n- Preserve context when customers edit.\n- Treat substitutions as a choice, not a footnote.\n- Pair conversion metrics with delivery quality.')],
'mednest':[
('Problem','Clinic staff should spend their attention on patients, not on navigating a busy dashboard. In this demo scenario, booking an appointment required moving between disconnected views while the queue continued to grow.\n\nThe challenge was to make scheduling faster without hiding the context needed to avoid mistakes.'),
('Research','The illustrative research plan follows front-desk tasks: identify the patient, find an appropriate slot, confirm the visit and handle changes. A task map highlights repeated data entry and points where staff must remember information between screens.\n\nThe assumptions in this fictional case need validation with real clinic teams. No actual patient data appears in the mockups.'),
('Ideation','I explored a calendar-first workspace, a patient-first search and a unified daily queue. The daily view provided the best starting point for common tasks, while search stayed available for returning patients.\n\nEarly sketches separated urgent alerts from routine status. Colour was paired with text so that state would not depend on colour recognition.'),
('Design','A consistent appointment panel keeps patient context, time and visit details together. The daily dashboard offers a clear queue, visible status and direct paths to the next task. Confirmation screens repeat the critical details before saving.\n\nThe mockups use invented names and appointment entries. This portfolio includes no clinic backend, medical records or real scheduling functionality.'),
('Results','The client-supplied fictional outcome is **3× faster appointment booking**. A real benchmark should time the same task, separate new and returning patients, and measure booking errors alongside speed.\n\nThe design deliberately leaves an explicit confirmation step. Saving a few seconds is not worth creating an incorrect appointment.'),
('Key learnings','- Put high-frequency tasks where the work already happens.\n- Faster is useful only when it is also correct.\n- State needs words as well as colour.\n- Design the interrupted workflow, not just the ideal one.')],
'yatra-lite':[
('Problem','A travel idea can happen anywhere, including on a train with an unreliable connection. This fictional PWA concept starts from that constraint: visitors need useful trip information before decorative content finishes loading.\n\nThe aim was to make discovering and comparing stays feel light without stripping away the joy of planning.'),
('Research','The demo research plan tests the key jobs on low-end phones and limited networks: find a destination, compare a stay and return to a saved option. It prioritises readable content, clear loading states and recovery after a connection drops.\n\nThese are proposed evaluation conditions, not a claim that a production travel product was tested.'),
('Ideation','I compared image-heavy discovery, text-first search and a compact editorial collection. The final concept combines a focused destination card with short, useful details. Secondary media follows the essential content.\n\nAn information budget for each screen helped remove repeated details and reserve attention for the next decision.'),
('Design','The concept uses small reusable components, predictable aspect ratios and restrained visual effects. Destination cards expose location, stay type and the action clearly. Loading and unavailable states use the same layout to reduce disruptive shifts.\n\nThe original mockups are static illustrations. No bookings, accounts, offline travel database or payments are implemented by this portfolio.'),
('Results','The supplied fictional metric is **1.2 seconds for the first load on 3G**. It describes the concept in the brief, not this portfolio’s measured load time. A real performance claim needs a device, network profile and a clearly named metric.\n\nThe proposed evaluation combines loading performance with task completion, so a lightweight screen still has to be useful.'),
('Key learnings','- Start with the slowest connection you need to support.\n- Reserve space before content arrives.\n- A smaller payload cannot compensate for unclear choices.\n- Always name the metric behind a performance claim.')]
}
for slug,sections in cases.items():Path('content/cases/'+slug+'.mdx').write_text('\n\n'.join('## '+h+'\n\n'+t for h,t in sections)+'\n')
articles={
'friction':'Not every extra step is a design failure. A confirmation before a consequential action can be useful; asking someone to repeat information rarely is. The question is whether a moment of friction helps a person make a better decision.\n\nStart with the risk, then decide how much attention the action deserves.',
'systems':'A component library becomes useful when the team agrees what it is for. Naming, examples and the freedom to ask “does this fit?” matter as much as variants.\n\nTreat documentation as an ongoing conversation between the people designing, building and using the product.',
'handoff':'The interesting questions often arrive when a design meets real data. A long name, an empty state or a slow connection can expose assumptions hidden in the polished screen.\n\nStay close to the build. A short conversation at the right moment saves more time than another page of specs.',
'research':'Ask about the last time, not the ideal future. What happened? What did you try? Where did you pause? What did you expect? What happened next?\n\nSpecific stories reveal the constraints that hypothetical preferences tend to hide.'}
for slug,body in articles.items():Path('content/articles/'+slug+'.mdx').write_text(body+'\n')
